  "text": "Solution 1 is wrong. Its key step, that complex conjugation forces $\\eta(D)=\\eta(B)=0$ for every homogeneous metric, is asserted rather than shown. Its conclusion that $\\bar\\nu=0$ would also make the invariant trivial on these spaces, which is unlikely to be the point of the question.\n\nSolutions 2 and 3 disagree with each other, and neither is well supported.\n\n- **Solution 2 (|ν̄| = 6)** relies on a parity argument that $\\bar\\nu$ must be even, and then on a coboundary computation giving $\\nu = \\chi(W) - 3\\sigma(W) = 6$. The parity claim is not established, and the coboundary and signature computations are sketched rather than verified.\n- **Solution 3 (|ν̄| = 3)** gives a formula for $\\bar\\nu$ and then asserts the $\\eta$-invariant computation without doing it. It is also in tension with Solution 2's parity claim.\n\nI cannot verify the Crowley–Goette–Nordström result from memory. I have a vague recollection that the Aloff–Wallach values are small, possibly ±1, but I am not confident of that, and it does not match any of the three proposed answers. Without a reliable source for the value, I cannot confirm that any of the proposed final answers is correct. The check would need the actual computation in Crowley–Goette–Nordström's paper on the $\\bar\\nu$-invariant.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
